### Answers to your Bonus Questions:

1 & 2. **Perspective & Curves:** You are perfectly correct that perspective makes a straight line look diagonal. Our algorithm calculates the "center of mass" (centroid) by averaging the position of the line across *every* row in the Region of Interest (ROI). If the track is straight but diagonal, the centroid sits perfectly in the middle of that diagonal slice. If a curve appears, the line bends sharply, pulling the average centroid further in that direction. The car dynamically calculates the severity of the curve based on how far the centroid shifts from the center!
3. **Looking Too Far Ahead:** Looking ahead gives the car "anticipation," allowing it to steer smoothly into curves instead of jerking violently. However, if it looks *too* far ahead, it will cut the corner early. This is precisely why we use a Region of Interest! By artificially ignoring the top 2/3rds of the image, we force the algorithm to only calculate the centroid for the road immediately in front of the car. If the car still cuts corners too early, the ultimate fix is to physically tilt the camera lens downwards toward the floor.

