I have bad news: **You physically cannot fix the inconsistent distance using only those two degrees of freedom.** 

Here is the physics of why:
When your car approaches the intersection perfectly straight, the stop line enters the top of the camera frame (far away) and instantly spans the entire 160-pixel width. The code sees `width > 40` and stops the car while it is still far away.
However, if the car is slightly angled (e.g., turning to correct its path), the line enters the camera view at an angle. The piece of the line visible at the top of the camera might only be 20 pixels wide! The car has to drive forward a few more centimeters until the line moves further down into the camera's view (getting wider) to hit the `> 40` threshold. The code then stops the car closer to the intersection!

Because the `width > 40` trigger depends heavily on the car's approach angle, the distance will always be inconsistent. 

**The Professional Solution: The Y-Coordinate Tripwire**
To solve this, we must add a 3rd degree of freedom. We will create a physical "Tripwire" on the camera frame. 
When the stop line enters the top of the screen (far away), the camera will identify it, color it gray so the PID ignores it (preventing a crash), but it will **NOT** trigger the stop command. It will wait until the car drives close enough that the line physically touches `y = 50` (near the bottom of the camera frame). 
This locks the stopping distance to exactly whatever physical distance corresponds to `y = 50` on your camera, giving you millimeter precision every time!

