### Analysis & Explanation: The "Boot-Blindness" Bug

You are completely correct. The introduction of Wi-Fi is causing the ESP32 to draw massive current spikes (up to 500mA) when it tries to authenticate with your router. If your voltage regulator struggles to supply this, the ESP32's voltage dips below 2.7V, triggering a **Brownout Reset**.

But why does a reset cause it to drive forward? 
If the car resets while parked on the intersection line, it takes the camera roughly **2 seconds** to boot up and adjust its auto-exposure. During those 2 seconds, the image is completely pitch black. 
Because it is pitch black, your tripwire logic (`total_black_pixels < 12000`) correctly ignores it as a false positive. However, because the image is black, no lane is detected (`line_lost = true`). When `line_lost` is true, the PID defaults to `BASE_SPEED` for both wheels. **The car blindly drives forward at full speed for 2 seconds while the camera boots!** By the time the camera can finally see, the car has already blown past the intersection.

### The Solution
We will implement a **Boot Safety Lock**. If the camera image is completely black (which only happens during the first few seconds of a boot/reset), we will physically lock the motors to `0` and refuse to drive. 

This means if the Wi-Fi crashes and resets the car, the car will simply freeze in place, wait 2 seconds for the camera to wake up, instantly see the intersection again, and re-latch the parking brake. You won't even notice it reset!

To help prevent the resets entirely, we can also instruct the Wi-Fi radio to run in low-power mode.

