### Diagnostic Analysis: The Two Fatal Flaws

You have correctly isolated two completely separate bugs that are combining to cause catastrophic failure.

**1. The Physical Polarity Inversion (Why it steers right instead of left)**
If you look at the `/pid` frame, see the centroid at `46` (Left side of the screen), and the car violently steers Right, **your motor wiring polarity is inverted relative to the math.** 
The PID calculates: `error = SETPOINT - centroid` (140 - 46 = +94). A positive error commands the right wheel to spin faster than the left. On a standard chassis, this pivots the car to the left. If your physical robot turns right, it means your physical right wheel is mapped to the left, or the wires are swapped. We can fix this instantly in software by inverting the error calculation!

**2. The Vision Bias (Why it randomly "hallucinates" and locks up)**
The original vision algorithm contained a scanning loop that read the pixels from Right to Left (`x = 319` down to `0`). It was designed to break on the *first* dark object it saw. This means if there is a tiny, faint shadow on the far right side of the screen, the algorithm instantly locks onto it and completely ignores the massive black line sitting on the left! This perfectly explains why it "sees something different" while moving. 

We need to rip out the biased Right-to-Left scanner and replace it with a **True Center of Mass** algorithm. It will average the X-coordinates of *all* black pixels in the frame, making it mathematically immune to stray shadows and noise!

